\title[Reversible radius-one automata over three letters]{Reversible radius-one cellular automata over three letters:\\ refutation of the single-coordinate classification,\\ a finite reversibility test, and the inverse-radius gap}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
A radius-one cellular automaton over a finite alphabet $A$ is given by a local rule $g\colon A^3 \to A$ acting on the cycle $\ZZ/n$ by $(G_n s)(i) = g(s(i-1),s(i),s(i+1))$. Call $g$ \emph{cycle-bijective} when $G_n$ is a bijection for every $n \ge 1$. The obvious cycle-bijective rules are the \emph{single-coordinate rules} --- one window cell followed by a permutation of $A$ --- of which there are exactly $18$ over a three-letter alphabet. We settle whether these exhaust the cycle-bijective rules, and the answer is a sharp dichotomy in the alphabet size. We prove that over three letters the classification is false, exhibiting eighteen counterexamples built from sign-twisted rules $g_{u,v}(a,b,c)=\sgn_u(a)\,b\,\sgn_v(c)$ over $\FF_3$, each an involution on every cycle; that the failure holds over every alphabet with at least three letters while the binary case is rigid, so the classification holds precisely for alphabets of size at most two; that inside the affine class over $\FF_3$ the classification is exactly right and the infinite test collapses to injectivity on the single cycle of length $8$, sharply; that in general injectivity descends to divisor lengths and a splicing argument on the pair graph reduces cycle-bijectivity to the lengths $1,\dots,q^4$, whence decidability and a single sufficient length $(q^4)!$; and finally that a reversible radius-one rule may require a strictly wider inverse, with an explicit rule of decoding width exactly four and no radius-one inverse automaton.
\end{abstract}

